for 13 years paul velez was the  friendliest face at unlv as a member of  campus police he was a detective  information officer and served as  protection for the unlv football and  basketball teams he brought that sense  of duty from his 20-year tenure with  nypd he also carries around a memory  that connects us all  this summer paul velez retired after 33  years of being a policeman he earned the  opportunity to bask in southern nevada  sunshine especially after his work on  the darkest day in american history the  lower manhattan was just a giant  dust cloud  so as we were coming  into the ferry terminal  we couldn't see anything  on the morning of september 11th the  world stared terrified at its tv officer  paul bellez put on his uniform and  blindly walked into catastrophe the pile  as it became known  you know just trying to find people it  was just long lines of cops and firemen  filling up buckets and just passing them  down to get rubble out what we  romanticize as heroism  paul and his colleagues saw with watery  eyes as hysteria and kept going one of  the most haunting sounds as we were on  that pile for hours was people were  calling and still trying to get in touch  with their loved ones and their cell  phones were still operating  so you heard cell phones going off and  constant beeping of the firefighters  rescue beacons were going off that were  trapped and deceased under there in a  city of 18 million people the terrorist  attacks hit hardest at home one velez  son as a cop and the other as a  firefighter so we head down to the ferry  terminal to get deployed to manhattan  and  um we're online waiting to get in and i  look over to my right  and there's my brother  getting on another boat and i still get  choked up thinking about it um and  i couldn't believe it you know like and  we ran over to each other and you know  we were like hey you know be careful you  know don't become a hero you know  get home alive after working 30 straight  hours a new york tough guy has never  been so happy to see his brother you  know you come through the door and  there's my mom and she's hugging me and  crying and  i'm like i don't know where sal is she  points in the living room  [Music]  and he's sleeping on the couch  the attack on 9 11 wasn't just one day  or one week or one tragedy in the months  to come it was just  funeral after funeral after funeral this  policeman lives every day with what he  hopes no one else has to see you don't  want it to become just another  you know  show on the history channel paul velez  has retired with his beautiful wife in  las vegas but in the fall of 2001 he  worked 12-hour shifts for six straight  weeks in the haze of hell  he reminds us of a broken city and  united nation  may we never forget  thank you to officer velez for his  service we'll be right back
 

